A raindrop is about a hundred times wider than a cloud droplet, so it holds about a million times more water. Getting from one to the other takes time.

In warm clouds, droplets bump into each other and merge, growing bigger and heavier until rising air can't hold them up. In tall, cold clouds, tiny [[@earth-weird-frost-flowers|ice crystals]] grow by stealing water vapour from the droplets around them. They fall as snowflakes, then melt into rain on the way down. Much of the rain that falls even on hot Indian summer days started as ice high above.

{{story:redwoods}}

A compass doesn't point exactly at the North Pole, the spot Earth spins around. It points to the magnetic north pole, which is somewhere else, and it moves. For most of the last century it sat in the islands of northern Canada. Since the 1990s it has been racing towards Siberia, at times by about 50 km a year.

That shift matters in surprising places. [[@airports|Airport runways]] are numbered by their compass direction. As magnetic north drifts, some airports have had to repaint their runway numbers. In 2011, the airport in Tampa, Florida, closed runways so workers could paint new numbers on them. Navigation systems in phones and planes use a model of the field that scientists update every five years.

{{story:sea-turtles}}

In 1987, two astronomers, David Jewitt and his student Jane Luu, began searching using telescopes on Mauna Kea, a tall volcano in [[@hawaii-islands|Hawaii]]. They took pictures of the same patch of sky over time and looked for faint dots that moved.

After five years, in 1992, they found one. It was the first Kuiper Belt object known after Pluto and its moon Charon. Since then, astronomers have found thousands more. NASA estimates there may be hundreds of thousands of icy bodies larger than 100 km across, and perhaps a trillion or more comets. A trillion is a [[@big-numbers|million million]], more than a hundred comets for every person on Earth.

{{story:big-numbers}}

Summer monarchs live just a few weeks. They hatch, feed, lay eggs on milkweed plants and die. But the ones born at the end of summer are different. They don't lay eggs straight away, and they live for about eight months. This super generation makes the long journey south.

In spring, the same butterflies fly north to Texas and nearby states, lay eggs and die. Their children continue north, and so do their grandchildren. It takes three or four generations to get all the way back to Canada. You can follow each stage, from egg to caterpillar to wings, in [[@butterflies-life|a butterfly's life]].

{{story:butterflies-life}}

Blow lots of bubbles into a cluster and they stop being round. Where two bubbles touch, they share a flat wall. Where three meet, the walls always join at angles of 120 degrees, like the arms of a three-pointed star. The Belgian scientist Joseph Plateau worked out these rules in the 1800s by dipping wire frames into soapy water.

Now look at a [[@honeycomb|honeycomb]]. Its cells are hexagons, and every corner has walls meeting at 120 degrees. Bees and bubbles are both finding a way to divide up space using the least material.

{{story:honeycomb}}

In 1901, an English engineer named Hubert Cecil Booth watched a demonstration of a machine that blew dust off train seats. Most of the dust just landed somewhere else. Booth thought sucking would be better. The story goes that he tested the idea by laying a handkerchief on a chair and sucking through it with his mouth. The cloth came away dirty.

Booth built a machine powered by an engine. It was so large it sat on a horse-drawn cart in the street, and long hoses went in through the windows of [[@london|London]] homes. Some wealthy households reportedly invited friends round to watch. He was even hired to clean the carpets at Westminster Abbey before the coronation of King Edward VII in 1902.

{{story:robots-home}}

Plays outdoors are very old. In medieval Europe, actors performed religious stories on wagons that rolled from one town square to the next. In Italy from the 1500s, travelling troupes of commedia dell'arte played on simple platforms in public squares, with masked characters and made-up dialogue.

India has its own long traditions of open-air performance, from the bahurupiya, who wanders in disguise, to folk forms like Tamasha and Nautanki. Ramlila plays, staged in open grounds before Dussehra, gather whole neighbourhoods.

{{story:indian-theatre}}

A Tanjore painting starts with a plank of wood. A piece of cloth is glued on top, then coated with a smooth paste of chalk and gum and rubbed until flat. The artist sketches the design, usually with a big central figure.

Next comes the clever part. Using a thicker paste, the painter builds up the crown, necklaces and borders so they stand out from the surface, a little like icing on a cake. Glass beads and stones are pressed in. Only then does the gold go on, followed by the paint.

{{story:gold-where}}

In most American animation, actors record first. Animators then listen and draw mouths, eyes and gestures to fit. Voice actors often make faces and wave their arms in the booth, because it changes how they sound. Some films set up cameras to film them, so animators can borrow their expressions.

Actors usually record alone, reading only their own lines. They may not hear the other characters until the film comes out. Japanese anime often flips the order. Actors record together, watching the scenes play on a screen and fitting their words to mouths that are already drawn.

{{story:voice}}

The Greeks did keep a sacred fire burning at Olympia during their Games, and relay races with torches were a real ancient sport. But the modern flame is much newer. It first appeared at the 1928 Amsterdam Olympics, burning in a tower.

The relay itself was dreamed up by Carl Diem, an organiser of the 1936 Berlin Games. More than 3,000 runners carried the flame about 3,000 km from Greece to Germany. Hitler's government used it, and the whole Olympics, as [[@propaganda|propaganda]] to show off Nazi Germany. The tradition survived anyway, and today it is used to celebrate peace.

{{story:ancient-olympia}}

Tennis began as jeu de paume, "game of the palm", in medieval France. Players at first hit a ball with their bare hands, then with gloves, then with rackets. It was played indoors in long stone halls, and kings loved it. Hundreds of courts were built in and around [[@paris|Paris]].

The name tennis probably comes from the French "tenez", meaning "take this" or "here you go", which a server called out. That version, now called real tennis, is still played on a few dozen indoor courts in Britain, France, the USA and Australia.

{{story:clocks}}

Through battles, alliances and marriages, Ranjit Singh brought the misls together. He took Multan in 1818 and Kashmir in 1819. In 1834 his forces captured Peshawar, at the foot of the Khyber Pass, an entry point that invaders had used for centuries.

He called his government the Sarkar-i-Khalsa, the government of the Khalsa, and issued coins in the name of the Sikh Gurus rather than his own. Visitors noted that he dressed simply and often sat cross-legged among his courtiers. His kingdom stretched from the Sutlej river to the mountains of Afghanistan.

{{story:punjab}}

That question has more than one answer. A popular story says a deadly plague swept the region, and the sultan promised to build a mosque where he prayed for it to end. The Charminar, the story goes, marks that spot.

Some historians think the plague story came later. They point out that a grand gateway at the heart of a planned city makes sense on its own. It may have marked the city's centre and the start of a new millennium in the Islamic calendar, which began around this time. Nobody has found a document from 1591 that settles it.

{{story:hyderabad}}

The princely state of Mysore was quick to bring in electricity. In 1902 it opened a hydroelectric power station at Shivanasamudra, on the Kaveri river. It was one of the first in Asia, and sent power about 140 km away to the Kolar gold mines.

Soon after, Bangalore became one of the first cities in India to light its streets with electric lamps, and Mysore followed. Covering the new palace in [[@light-bulb|light bulbs]] was a way of saying the kingdom was modern.

{{story:light-bulb}}

Assam's tea gardens produce about half of India's [[@tea|tea]]. Its strong, malty taste is used in breakfast teas around the world. Assam is also home to Kaziranga National Park, where about two-thirds of the world's one-horned rhinos live.

{{story:rhino-kaziranga}}

The hills are full of [[@bamboo|bamboo]], used for houses, baskets, bridges and even food, as [[@northeast-food|bamboo shoots]]. Manipur's [[@india-wonder-loktak|Loktak Lake]] has floating islands of plants called phumdis. One of them forms a national park, home to the rare sangai deer. In the eastern forests live red pandas, [[@hornbills|hornbills]] and clouded leopards. The bhut jolokia, or ghost pepper, from Assam and Nagaland, was once named the hottest [[@chillies|chilli]] in the world.

In 1814, Sake Dean Mahomed, an entrepreneur from Patna, opened a bathhouse in Brighton, England. He offered "shampooing", an Indian-style massage with steam and oils, and claimed it could ease aches and pains. It became so fashionable that he was appointed "shampooing surgeon" to King George IV.

The word came from Hindi *champo*, "press". Only later in the 1800s did "shampoo" come to mean washing the hair, and later still the liquid you wash it with. Dean Mahomed has another claim to fame. In 1794, he published his travels, making him one of the first Indians to write a book in English.

{{story:soap-history}}

Papad dough must be stiff and smooth. Makers pound or knead it for a long time, sometimes on a stone. A pinch of papad khar, an alkaline salt, is often added. It helps the papad expand and crisp up when cooked.

The dough is rolled into thin circles, sometimes just a millimetre thick. Expert makers can roll dozens an hour, each one perfectly round. The discs are then laid out on cloth or mats in the sun.

{{story:cooperatives}}
